class Solution {
public:
    int trap(vector<int>& height) 
    {
        int n=height.size();
        int lmax=INT_MIN;
        int rmax=INT_MIN;
        int res=0;
        for(int i=1;i<n-1;i++)
        {
            int lmax = INT_MIN;   // reset here
            int rmax = INT_MIN;   // reset here
           for(int j=0;j<i;j++)
           {
              lmax=max(lmax,height[j]);
           }
           for(int k=i+1;k<n;k++)
           {
              rmax=max(rmax,height[k]);
           }

           res += max(0,min(lmax,rmax)-height[i]);
        }
        return res;
    }
};

class Solution {
public:
    int trap(vector<int>& height) 
    {
       int n=height.size();
       int l=0;
       int r=n-1;
       int res=0;
       int lmax=INT_MIN;
       int rmax=INT_MIN;

       while(l<r)
       {
           lmax = max(lmax,height[l]);
           rmax = max(rmax,height[r]);

           if(lmax<rmax)
           {
              res += min(lmax,rmax)-height[l];
              l++;
           } 
           else
           {
              res += min(lmax,rmax)-height[r];
              r--;
           }
       }  
       return res; 
    }

    Brute Force:
Use a loop for each index i (excluding first and last).
For every index, try to compute water stored at that position.

At each step:
• Find left max from 0 → i-1.
• Find right max from i+1 → n-1.
• Height of water = min(lmax, rmax) (smaller one limits water).
• Water stored = min(lmax, rmax) - height[i].
• Add to result using max(0, …) to avoid negative.

Key Idea
Water at any index is limited by the smaller of left and right maximum heights.

Important Trick
Always take min(lmax, rmax) because water spills over the shorter boundary.

Important Note
Reset lmax and rmax for every index.

Time: O(N²)
Space: O(1)


Use two pointers l (start) and r (end).
Maintain running maximums from both sides.

At each step:
• Update lmax = max(lmax, height[l])
• Update rmax = max(rmax, height[r])
• Compare lmax and rmax:

If lmax < rmax → left side is limiting
• Water = lmax - height[l]
• Add to result
• Move l++
Else → right side is limiting
• Water = rmax - height[r]
• Add to result
• Move r--

Key Idea
Water level is decided by the smaller of leftMax and rightMax.

Important Trick
Process the side with smaller max, because it is the bottleneck.

Why it works
If lmax < rmax, then water at l depends only on lmax (right side is guaranteed taller).

Common Mistake
Do NOT compare height[l] and height[r] → compare lmax and rmax.

Time: O(N)
Space: O(1)

